Z||000000||Shiner||11-05-1999||02:31 AM||||Does GM still make the all aluminum 60* block?  I know it was expensive when they did, but this sure would be nice if you were going to totally overhaul the engine anyway. Any info is appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88 Fiero||11-05-1999||01:18 PM||||If you are looking for a 3.1 aluminum engine..I think pontiac build that engine on the Pontiac Transport and the Pontiac 6000 STE cars....||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||11-05-1999||03:15 PM||||Shiner :  I believe you are referring to the <BR>10051141 — Bow Tie Aluminum 3.0L Block. <BR>Even if they discontinued it as I believe they did, you could probably find some on the shelves collecting dust.  With a price of over $6000 for the block and the crank bearings required, it was not a big seller.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||11-05-1999||08:25 PM||||With the talk of the "Bowtie" aluminum block there might be a reason why beside the price its been sitting on the shelf. The 3.4 block used in the Camaro/Firebird in the mid 90s has been utilized by Fiero Motorsports as a direct replacement for the 2.8. The block is much more reenforced than the 2.8 and is rated at 160 hp with 200 torque. Fiero Motorsports has done more than 50 of them now with fantastic results. It is an iron block that uses the Fiero heads. (Interesting note that the 2.8 found in the Camaro/Firebird had smaller valves than the Fiero).<BR>Conclusion: Keep the 6 grand (that much???) and get a 3.4 and trick it out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||11-06-1999||02:37 AM||||Yes, they DO make it.  It's in GM's catalogue for 1999.  The part number is 10051141<P>It's still in production... trust me.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||11-06-1999||03:06 AM||||Actually the aluminum block does not cost $6k.  It is more around $3k.  I was interested in one of these a while back as I was going to buy from a private individual and he told me the block cost $3k.  If in doubt or just curious as to how much it cost go down to your local Gm dealer and have them check to see how much it will cost.  Post it here and then we will know for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||11-06-1999||05:01 AM||||The $6k I was referring to was not for the block alone, this includes the block specific crank bearings and crank as I stated. But, since I left out a comma in my previous post, it was easily misunderstood.  Last I checked (~ 1 yr ago), the block alone was close to $4000.  I can see no real use in being critical over something that is targeted primarily at those in a professional racing field.  Maybe more "do" and less dream would be good idea?||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||11-06-1999||04:40 PM||||I must say I agree with MWBackus's last statement "more do and less dream."  I see a lot of people posting stuff like: "Where can I get a twin turbo kit? How do I supercharge this 4-cyl?  How do I get 2,000,000,000 horsepower out of my 2.8 V6"  I'm going a little overboard here, but you get the idea. Just work with what you have, and be REALISTIC.  If you want to install a turbo kit, you better know what the hell you're doing first!  I know it can be done, but people just expect to stop by their local walmart store and pick up a supercharger to throw in. I get the feeling that most of the people who post this kind of stuff have never even pulled a valve cover.  I'm not targetting anyone in particular, but it's just something I've noticed. ||||reg||3
Z||000008||GT Bastard||11-06-1999||05:07 PM||||BTW, I was thinking about that aluminum block too, until I saw the price.  Woulda been a cool idea.||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||11-06-1999||10:36 PM||||The aluminum block is only 47 pounds lighter than a production block, three grand can buy you a lot of performance parts for the block you already have! ||||reg||1
Z||000010||David||11-07-1999||01:06 PM||||About a year ago, Nov 27,1998 to be exact, I priced the 10050041 aluminum block from my local GM dealer and the price was $4,534.06(US).  This is about $100 per pound,  and didn't seem like a good use on my money.  I would agree with others that the money can be better spent in other areas. Forty-five hundred dollars can put a V8 in your car.<p>[This message has been edited by David (edited 11-07-1999).]||||reg||1
